from pathlib import Path
import json,html,base64,re,textwrap
root=Path('.')
D=json.loads((root/'data/results.json').read_text());media=json.loads((root/'publication/media.json').read_text());meta=json.loads((root/'publication/archive-metadata.json').read_text())
def p(s):return '<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>'+s+'</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n'
def h(s):return '<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class="wp-block-heading">'+s+'</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n'
def code(s):return '<!-- wp:code -->\n<pre class="wp-block-code"><code>'+html.escape(s)+'</code></pre>\n<!-- /wp:code -->\n'
def table(headers,rows):return '<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+str(x)+'</th>' for x in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+str(x)+'</td>' for x in r)+'</tr>' for r in rows)+'</tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n'
def image(key):
 m=media[key];caption=m['caption']
 if key+'-still' in media:caption+=' <a href="'+media[key+'-still']['source_url']+'">View the static fallback</a>.'
 return '<!-- wp:image '+json.dumps({'id':m['id'],'sizeSlug':'full','linkDestination':'none'})+' -->\n<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img src="'+m['source_url']+'" alt="'+html.escape(m['alt_text'],quote=True)+'" class="wp-image-'+str(m['id'])+'"/><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">'+caption+'</figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n'
names={'aligned':'Aligned bundles','isotropic':'Disordered','hierarchical':'Hierarchical','modular':'Modular','loops':'Diagonal reinforcement','bottleneck':'Bottlenecked','heterogeneous':'Degree heterogeneous'}
def summary(f,m):return next(x for x in D['summaries'] if x['family']==f and x['mode']==m)
def value(r):return f"{r['meanWork']*1000:.3f} ± {r['sdWork']*1000:.3f}"+(f" †{r['censored']}/5" if r['censored'] else '')
main=p('<strong>No single collagen-network connectivity or spectral statistic emerged as a universal toughness score.</strong> In this original 450-run local benchmark, diagonal reinforcement absorbed the most work in X tension, aligned bundles carried the greatest tensile peak force, and the ordering changed under shear and constrained acceleration. That is a result about a small, hypothetical 2D model—not an experimentally validated ranking of collagen tissues under extreme loads.')
main+=p('The interactive <em>Fracture Atlas</em> explorer, complete source, numerical records, equations, logs and validation checks are preserved in the <a href="__SUPPLEMENT_URL__">linked reproducibility supplement</a>. The animations below are drawn from computed coordinates and broken-link states. They are not generated illustrations of imagined performance.')+image('benchmark')
main+=h('The comparison first fixes what the load means')
main+=p('Each baseline specimen is a unit square with nominal unit thickness, 36 nodes, 82 edges and the same total collagen-bundle volume, 0.06. The seven architectures share initial node positions for each seed. All have mean degree 4.556 and cycle rank 47. “Diagonal reinforcement” describes the placement of redundant paths, not more cycles or extra collagen. Five seeds—17, 29, 43, 71 and 101—are retained rather than selecting a favorable realization.')
main+=p('The generator includes aligned bundles, a jittered disordered network, a trunk-and-branch motif, four spatial modules, diagonal reinforcement, one bridge across the X midline, and two high-degree hubs. Segment crossings do not create junctions. Forces come from a nonlinear axial law and selected angular hinges, with relaxation before rupture. Crimp, bending, rupture thresholds and sacrificial links are explicit hypothetical controls; no collagen type or molecular crosslink identity receives an invented measured parameter.')
main+=table(['Protocol','Physical specification'],[['Tension / compression','Normal displacement on opposing X or Y faces; lateral freedom or confinement stated explicitly. Quasistatic relaxation, not an assigned finite loading rate.'],['Shear','Bottom fixed, top translated horizontally at fixed height; side faces free.'],['Confined / biaxial compression','Prescribed skeleton deformation, with a 30% strain cap. No fluid pressure field or drainage.'],['Constrained pulse','Uniform body force m·a(t); left edge fixed. Dimensionless density 1, a(t)=0.018 sin(πt/8) for 0≤t≤8; observe to t=16.'],['Free acceleration','Identical body force per mass and time history, with all nodes free.']])
main+=p('Length, material modulus and density scales are L₀, E₀ and ρ₀; time is t₀=L₀√(ρ₀/E₀). No absolute values are fitted. The simulations therefore cannot state a safe g-load, an extreme pressure in MPa, a shock tolerance or a tissue failure limit.')
main+=h('Toughness, strength and stiffness separate')
main+=p('Quasistatic work is the integrated boundary reaction up to the first sampled state with 20% collagen volume ruptured, loss of an intact path between the loaded faces, or a substantial post-peak loss of force capacity. The force-drop rule requires reaction below half its running maximum, strain above 0.08 and a peak above 10⁻⁵. It can detect softening without rupture. Work includes both stored and dissipated energy; strength is peak reaction; initial stiffness is the first secant slope. A capped specimen has not supplied a complete work-to-failure measurement. There is no crack-area model, so this is <strong>work density, not fracture energy in J/m²</strong>.')
main+=table(['Architecture','X tension','Shear','Constrained pulse'],[[names[f],value(summary(f,'tension')),value(summary(f,'shear')),value(summary(f,'pulse'))] for f in names])
main+=p('Values are work density ×10⁻³ E₀, mean ± sample standard deviation; the SD is not a confidence interval. † records the number of capped runs. Pulse work has a different operational definition: stored elastic energy plus irreversible and damping losses, excluding kinetic energy. These are protocol-dependent comparisons, not one common material constant.')
main+=p('In X tension, diagonal reinforcement averaged 0.609×10⁻³ E₀ work to the criterion and 58.4% strain to that criterion. Aligned bundles averaged 0.486×10⁻³ E₀ and 20.7%, but had nearly twice the mean peak reaction: 4.865×10⁻³ versus 2.492×10⁻³. Those two families form the mean work/strength Pareto frontier in this sample. The work distributions overlap other families, and five seeds do not establish a universal rank order.')+image('tension')
main+=h('A bottleneck can change meaning when the grips change')
main+=p('The X-bottlenecked family is weak in X tension but has the largest sampled mean shear work, 0.315×10⁻³ E₀, close to the disordered family at 0.301×10⁻³ E₀. Shear uses the top and bottom faces, so the X-directed weak cut does not play the same role. This is a geometry-and-boundary counterexample, not a recommendation to add bottlenecks. The aligned family stays very soft in shear and never reaches the criterion in the tested window.')+image('shear')
main+=h('Pressure resistance remains an unresolved toughness question')
main+=p('At a 30% compression cap, all 35 laterally confined runs and 33 of 35 biaxial runs reached the cap without the operational failure criterion. None of the 105 baseline compression runs ruptured collagen. Some unconfined and biaxial runs met the force-drop criterion through softening. Hierarchical and disordered networks showed greater mean confined-compression work than the bottlenecked network, but <strong>greater resistance over this window does not establish greater failure toughness</strong>.')
main+=p('The animation imposes confined solid-skeleton compression. Uniform hydrostatic stress, a differential hydraulic pressure and pore pressure inside a hydrated material are distinct problems. This model has no permeability, fluid mass balance, drainage, cavitation, out-of-plane buckling or shock equation of state. It cannot identify a collagen matrix that is safest under extreme hydrated pressure.')+image('pressure')
main+=h('Acceleration loads a constrained matrix; free acceleration translates it')
main+=p('Among pulse families with all five criteria observed, degree-heterogeneous networks had the highest mean internalized work, 1.604×10⁻³ E₀. The modular family’s mixed endpoint mean was higher, but one specimen was capped, so it cannot be declared the pulse toughness winner. Pulse duration, density and damping remain part of the result. The score is taken at first criterion crossing; the animation continues through the full observation window.')+image('pulse')
main+=p('Free acceleration was the essential control: across 35 baseline runs, maximum fiber strain was about 1.26×10⁻¹³, with zero rupture and material work near numerical zero. The supplied external work became rigid-body kinetic energy. Clamping the left edge under the same acceleration generated deformation and failure. A stress specification needs mass density, size, supports and a time history; acceleration alone is insufficient.')+image('free-vs-clamped')
main+=h('Which connectivity and spectral statistics actually helped?')
main+=p('Mean degree and cycle rank cannot explain a fixed-budget sample in which they do not vary. Degree variance had essentially no X-tension association with work (Spearman ρ=−0.029). Unweighted Laplacian algebraic connectivity λ₂ was only modestly associated with X-tension work (ρ=0.312) and weakly associated with shear work (ρ=0.120). Maximum edge betweenness and reference soft-mode localization had negative X-tension associations, −0.429 and −0.438. These are descriptive, family-confounded relationships.')
main+=p('The study distinguishes unweighted, stiffness-weighted and normalized graph Laplacians, and separately computes a supported mechanical Hessian with bending and geometry. It also records k-core, exact unit-capacity edge connectivity, clustering, spatial-quadrant modularity, geometric anisotropy, segment lengths and force localization. The reference Hessian uses X-tension supports; its transfer to other modes is itself limited. The bulk Maxwell value z≈4 is only a central-force counting guide in 2D, not a sufficient rigidity criterion for a finite bending-stabilized specimen.')
main+=p('An exact failure of a direction-blind scalar score is the same aligned graph loaded in X versus Y: every graph eigenvalue is unchanged, while the mechanical response differs drastically. The Y case stays capped and nearly floppy, so comparing its recorded endpoint with X failure as two measured toughnesses would be wrong.')
main+=table(['X-tension predictor','Held-out seeds R²','Held-out families R²'],[['Mean-only baseline','−0.000','−0.329'],['Graph spectral features','0.242','−3.464'],['Geometry features','0.187','−1.745'],['Mechanical features','0.469','−0.040'],['Combined features','0.802','−0.570']])
main+=p('Ridge fits predict log work with training-only standardization, fixed penalty 1, and seeds 71/101 held out. Mechanical features include an early secant stiffness, so that predictor assumes a mechanical test. Every feature set had negative R² when an entire architecture was held out. The most useful answer is therefore conditional: combine geometry, supports and early mechanics, retain uncertainty, and test transfer explicitly. A spectral gap alone did not generalize.')
main+=h('Numerical checks and the assumptions that still matter')
main+=p('The run contains 280 topology/protocol cases, 24 independent edge-count cases, 42 composition hypotheses, six subfailure cycles, 59 numerical/regularizer checks, 27 pulse duration/density cases and 12 finite-size cases. All 350 quasistatic runs met their residual tolerance. Nine physical/numerical checks passed, and seven tensile reruns reproduced saved work within rounding. This is implementation evidence, not experimental validation.')
main+=p('Halving the load increment changed uncensored seed-17 tension/shear work by as much as 11.6%; event resolution is not fully converged. Tightening the tolerance reduced the tiny aligned-shear work by about 96%, exposing an unresolved nearly floppy response. Halving pulse time steps in three families changed internalized work by at most 0.13%; baseline pulse energy-balance error was at most 0.062% of external work.')
main+=p('Some fibers otherwise collapse to zero length, so an <strong>artificial compression regularizer</strong> is used below 40% of reference segment length. Its activation is flagged. Halving or doubling its coefficient changed the tested seed-17 tensile/shear work by at most 1.6%, but that narrow sensitivity does not make it a real contact law. Rupture strains, crimp, damping and sacrificial-link rules remain imposed. The elastin-like background adds material and survives collagen rupture; its extra work is not a pure topology improvement. Hydration and proteoglycan pressure are deliberately left uncalibrated.')
main+=h('Where experimental evidence begins—and ends')
main+=p('Primary studies motivate non-affine bending/stretching mechanics and strain-induced rigidity: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1504258112">Licup et al. (2015)</a> and <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/nphys3628">Sharma et al. (2016)</a>. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat1428">Fantner et al. (2005)</a> motivate sacrificial-bond dissipation in a mineralized composite, without calibrating our link law. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1039/C8SM02143K">Pressure-step experiments in collagen hydrogels</a> motivate the poroelastic physics that this skeleton model omits. The supplement distinguishes measured inputs (none), literature context and hypothetical controls.')
main+=p('The earlier <a href="https://humanavianaisymbiosis.wordpress.com/2026/10/31/scienceclaw-collagen-brev-alchemi-results/">H100 fragment relaxations and L4 MACE proxy scans</a> remain separate. Approximate glucosepane, a neutral pyridinoline proxy, model sensitivity and boundary minima prevent treating those curvatures as native-crosslink rupture mechanics or tissue calibration. No molecular k or k_rel entered this explorer, and no new cloud instance or molecular workload was started.')
main+=p('<a href="__SUPPLEMENT_URL__"><strong>Open the complete source, data and methods supplement</strong></a>. The local explorer runs without package installation. Its interface lets readers compare the seven families, inspect all loading modes, change hypothetical assumptions and export the current pair. The WordPress article hosts the verified plots and animations; it does not execute the local JavaScript application.')
Path('publication/main.html').write_text(main)

def inline(s):
 s=html.escape(s);s=re.sub(r'\[([^\]]+)\]\((https?://[^)]+)\)',r'<a href="\2">\1</a>',s);s=re.sub(r'\*\*(.+?)\*\*',r'<strong>\1</strong>',s);s=re.sub(r'`([^`]+)`',r'<code>\1</code>',s);return s
def markdown(src):
 blocks=[];paras=[];fenced=None;tab=[]
 def flush():
  if paras:blocks.append(p(inline(' '.join(paras))));paras.clear()
  if tab:blocks.append(table([inline(v.strip()) for v in tab[0].strip('|').split('|')],[[inline(v.strip()) for v in row.strip('|').split('|')] for row in tab[2:]]));tab.clear()
 for line in src.splitlines():
  if line.startswith('```'):
   if fenced is None:flush();fenced=[]
   else:blocks.append(code('\n'.join(fenced)));fenced=None
  elif fenced is not None:fenced.append(line)
  elif line.startswith('|'):tab.append(line)
  elif not line.strip():flush()
  elif line.startswith('#'):flush();blocks.append(h(inline(line.lstrip('# '))))
  else:paras.append(line[2:] if line.startswith('- ') else line)
 flush();return ''.join(blocks)
